<div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim</div>
<div class="halacha-title">Hilchos Devarim HaNohagin B'Seudah — Halachah 4<br><span style="font-size:1rem;font-weight:400;">Part 2: §§ 13–23 — <i>Ushpizin</i> through Bread and Eating</span></div>
<div class="lm-subtitle">Continuation of <span class="v">"Tisha Tikkunin Yakirin"</span> <span class="sr">(<span class="lr">LM I, 20</span>)</span></div>

<!-- §13 -->
<div class="sn">§ 13 — The Seven <i>Ushpizin</i>: Seven Shepherds Who Revealed <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">The seven <span class="h">Ushpizin</span> — the seven shepherds who enter the <span class="h">sukkah</span> — are those through whom the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> was revealed. <span class="kc">Avraham</span> was the first: Hashem told him <span class="v">"go forth from your land… to the Land"</span> — revealing that there exists a Land holier than all others, and that whoever wishes to know Hashem must go there. Then He promised the Land to his children. <span class="kc">Yitzchak</span> and <span class="kc">Yaakov</span> received the same promise. <span class="kc">Moshe</span> and <span class="kc">Aharon</span> brought Israel out of Mitzrayim — and the entire purpose of the Exodus was to bring them to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. <span class="kc">Yosef</span> cherished <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and adjured Israel to bring his bones there — upon which the redemption from Mitzrayim depended (Zohar). <span class="kc">Dovid HaMelech</span> fought Hashem's wars and conquered <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> in its fullness: <span class="v">"you shall dwell in the Land… and He will give you rest from all your enemies."</span></div>

<div class="p">Through all seven, <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness was revealed. Therefore they enter the <span class="h">sukkah</span> — for by their power we merit to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. On the seventh day (Hoshana Rabbah) = Dovid — then we merit the full conquest. Therefore the <span class="h">sukkah</span> is called <span class="v">"Dovid's <span class="h">sukkah</span>"</span>: <span class="v">"on that day I will raise up the fallen <span class="h">sukkah</span> of Dovid."</span></div>

<div class="p">On Hoshana Rabbah we dig anew the wells of Torah-water, as the Zohar states — and we read <span class="h">Tehillim</span>, which Dovid composed entirely through mercy and supplication as a pauper: <span class="v">"incline Your ear, Hashem, answer me, for I am poor and destitute."</span> Through this he conquered <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. We also read <span class="h">Mishneh Torah</span> [Deuteronomy] — nearly every <span class="h">parshah</span> speaks of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>: how Hashem desired to bring them immediately; how the <span class="h">meraglim</span> slandered the Land; and how observance of Torah leads to inheriting it: <span class="v">"and you shall keep and do in the Land"</span>; <span class="v">"in order that you may live long in the Land"</span>; <span class="v">"do what is right and good, and come and inherit the Land."</span></div>

<!-- §14 -->
<div class="sn">§ 14 — Hoshana Rabbah: The <i>Aravos</i> Beaten Alone</div>

<div class="p">On Hoshana Rabbah, the <span class="h">aravos</span> [willows] are taken alone and beaten. Throughout Sukkos, the <span class="h">aravos</span> were bound with the <span class="h">lulav</span> — the wicked rectified only when attached to the <span class="h">tzadik</span>. Now, on the final day, the <span class="h">tzadik</span> has rectified them so thoroughly that <span class="kc">they can stand on their own</span> — serving Hashem and drawing Torah by themselves. This is the essential <span class="h">tikkun</span>: the person returns to Hashem through his own <span class="h">bechirah</span> [free choice], not merely through the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s power.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">aravos</span> need beating on the ground to sweeten the judgments that clung to them when they were far away — so they are fully rectified. From them arise <span class="h">ma'alin min nukvin</span> [feminine waters rising] to the <span class="h">Shechinah</span>, who takes pride in them: <span class="mx">"See what children I bring before You!"</span> Through this, the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> unites on Shemini Atzeres = conquering <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. For the essential conquest is through all the wicked returning in <span class="h">teshuvah</span> = ingathering of exiles to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>.</div>

<!-- §15 -->
<div class="sn">§ 15 — <i>Shemini Atzeres</i> and <i>Simchas Torah</i>: Ascending to <i>Eretz Yisrael</i> in Joy</div>

<div class="p">On Shemini Atzeres and Simchas Torah, we ascend to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> in joy. Therefore the joy is immense — for one must ascend to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> with joy: <span class="v">"that You bring us up in joy to our Land"</span>; <span class="v">"they shall come to Tziyon in song, with eternal joy upon their heads"</span>; <span class="v">"for in joy you shall go forth."</span> On Shemini Atzeres, the <span class="h">Malchus</span> reaches its full perfection and unites with her Beloved = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s complete holiness.</div>

<!-- §16 -->
<div class="sn">§ 16 — <i>V'Zos HaBerachah</i> and Moshe's Passing</div>

<div class="p">We read <span class="h">V'Zos HaBerachah</span> — the Torah's conclusion. Through the Torah we completed, we merit <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. We also read Moshe's passing: he departed because of the error at the rock, and through his departure he rectified the blemish — <span class="mx">"what was diminished through them was completed through them"</span> — the additional holiness above through his <span class="h">neshamah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Through this rectification, we will soon merit to come to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> through <span class="h">Mashiach Tzidkeinu</span> — who is Moshe himself: <span class="mx">"what was, is what will be."</span> He was the first redeemer and he will be the final one. Through his departure he merits to be <span class="h">Mashiach</span>. He will then draw Torah through mercy and supplication, gather our exiles, return everyone in complete <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, and bring us all to <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we read Moshe's passing on Simchas Torah — to merit <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> through his departure. This is the completion of the <span class="h">tikkun</span> on Shemini Atzeres/Simchas Torah: <span class="h">Malchus</span> is perfected, united with her Beloved = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness.</div>

<div class="sep">✦ ✦ ✦</div>

<!-- §17 -->
<div class="sn">§ 17 — Application to Eating: Why <i>HaMotzi</i> Is Supreme</div>

<div class="p">From this Torah, the significance of the meal and <span class="h">Birchas HaMotzi</span> is explained — why it is the most important of all blessings, precedes all others, and exempts everything that comes during the meal.</div>

<div class="p">The essential holiness of eating derives from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> — from there all sustenance and nourishment flows: <span class="mx">"all lands drink from the residue of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>."</span> The holiness of bread comes from there: all <span class="h">mitzvos</span> pertaining to bread (<span class="h">terumos</span>, <span class="h">ma'asros</span>, <span class="h">challah</span>) are <span class="h">mitzvos</span> dependent on the Land. And the essential holiness distinguishing <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> from all other lands is precisely in the realm of eating — for <span class="kc">all other <span class="h">mitzvos</span> apply both in the Land and outside, but <span class="h">mitzvos</span> dependent on the Land apply only there</span>, and these are the <span class="h">mitzvos</span> of eating.</div>

<div class="p">Every <span class="h">berachah</span> on food draws holiness from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. All <span class="h">Birchos HaNehenin</span> were derived by our Sages from <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span>: <span class="mx">"if one blesses afterward, all the more so beforehand!"</span> And the essential <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> is to bless Hashem <span class="kc">for <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span></span>: <span class="v">"you shall eat and be satisfied, and bless Hashem your G-d for the Land."</span></div>

<div class="p">At first glance this is astonishing — what does <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> have to do with the blessing on food? But through the above, it is perfectly clear: the essential nourishment derives only from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>; the holiness drawn through the <span class="h">berachah</span> is the holiness of the Land. And every <span class="h">berachah</span> — "who brings forth bread from the earth," "all was created by His word," "who creates the fruit of the tree" — reveals that Hashem created that thing. Through revealing this, we reveal <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness — for as Rashi explains on <span class="v">"In the beginning"</span>: <span class="v">"the power of His deeds He told His people, to give them the inheritance of the nations"</span> — the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> rests upon knowing that He created the world. <span class="mx">"He created it; by His will He gave it to them, and by His will He took it and gave it to us."</span></div>

<!-- §18 -->
<div class="sn">§ 18 — Bread = Torah = Water and Fire</div>

<div class="p">To draw the holiness of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, one must draw Torah through all the <span class="h">tikkunim</span> described: pouring out the heart like water before Hashem, receiving hot words like fiery coals from the Upper Heart, connecting with the souls of the assembly. All this must be drawn during eating — for eating's holiness derives from <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>.</div>

<div class="p">Bread = Torah: <span class="v">"come, eat of my bread"</span>; <span class="v">"from afar she brings her bread."</span> The life-force in food = letters of Torah clothed within. Whoever eats in holiness draws the Torah's holiness through eating: <span class="v">"Your Torah is within my innards."</span></div>

<div class="p">Bread is only called <span class="h">lechem</span> when kneaded with <span class="kc">water</span> and baked in <span class="kc">fire</span>. These water and fire derive from the <span class="h">tzadikim</span>'s water and fire: pouring out the heart like water (= supplication before the teaching), then receiving hot words like fiery coals (= Torah illuminations from the Upper Heart). From there comes the power of the water and fire in bread. <span class="kc">Bread sustains the heart</span> — as our Sages found in Torah, Prophets, and Writings: <span class="mx">"bread sustains the heart."</span> For bread = the Torah that flows from the Upper Heart; therefore it has the power to sustain the heart below. <span class="mx">"A meal is called a meal only because of the bread."</span></div>

<div class="p">Therefore Hashem commanded <span class="h">Birchas HaMazon</span> on bread, with the essential blessing on <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>: <span class="v">"you shall eat and be satisfied, and bless for the Land."</span> The Zohar extols the meal's holiness extensively — ten <span class="h">tikkunim</span> in the meal, corresponding to the ten levels of holiness in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. <span class="kc">Fortunate is one who merits to taste eating bread like this.</span></div>

<div class="p" style="font-style:italic;color:var(--section-num);">And through this, one can understand some illumination in the wondrous story recorded among us — about the <span class="h">tzadik</span> who merited, through eating bread, the receiving of Torah. [See there and understand.]</div>

<!-- §19 -->
<div class="sn">§ 19 — Satiation Through the <i>Tzadik</i>: Shabbos and <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">The essential satiation from bread = the <span class="h">tzadik</span> = Yosef = the seven years of plenty. <span class="v">"The <span class="h">tzadik</span> eats to satisfy his soul."</span> Shabbos eating = the source of blessing and satiation, as <span class="h">Rabbainu</span> wrote. <span class="h">Shabbos</span> and <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> are one aspect — as the Yerushalmi lists seven distinctions: between the seventh day and the six workdays, between <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> and other lands, between Israel and the nations. All are parallel: <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>, <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, and <span class="h">Yisrael</span> are all holy; the nations, outside the Land, and weekdays are all <span class="h">chol</span>. Therefore essential satiation is in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>: <span class="v">"you will eat your bread to satiation and dwell securely in your land."</span> <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> receives from the <span class="h">tzadik</span>: <span class="v">"the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> shall inherit the Land."</span></div>

<!-- §20 -->
<div class="sn">§ 20 — Why Bread Exempts Everything in the Meal</div>

<div class="p">When the <span class="h">tzadik</span> draws Torah, he connects with all the souls of the assembly. From these two aspects — the <span class="h">tzadik</span> himself and the souls he connects with — all life-force of all foods flows. <span class="kc">Bread</span> (= the essential satiation) derives from the <span class="h">tzadik</span> himself — the source of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness, the root of satiation. The <span class="kc">other foods</span> in the meal derive from the many souls the <span class="h">tzadik</span> connects with during the teaching.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, when other foods come on their own, we bless <span class="h">Borei Nefashos Rabbos</span> — "who creates many souls" — for their life-force derives from the many souls. But when they come during a meal, <span class="kc">bread exempts them</span> — they are secondary to bread, whose life-force comes from the <span class="h">tzadik</span> (the essential), who connects all the souls to himself. All are secondary to him.</div>

<!-- §21 -->
<div class="sn">§ 21 — Ten <i>Mitzvos</i> in Bread: Ten Levels of <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">There are ten <span class="h">mitzvos</span> in bread — therefore one places both hands (ten fingers) on the bread during the <span class="h">berachah</span>. There are ten words in <span class="h">Birchas HaMotzi</span>; ten words in <span class="v">"a land of wheat and barley…"</span>; ten <span class="h">tikkunim</span> in the meal (Zohar). All correspond to the <span class="kc">ten levels of holiness in <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span></span> — from which the essential holiness of eating (especially bread) derives.</div>

<!-- §22 -->
<div class="sn">§ 22 — Bread as Warfare: <i>Lechem</i> = <i>Milchamah</i></div>

<div class="p">Every <span class="h">berachah</span> reveals that Hashem created the world — thus revealing the Ten Utterances of Creation. From these = the ten levels of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. The Torah consists of the Ten Commandments, through which the Ten Utterances are revealed: <span class="mx">"the whole world hung in the balance until the sixth of Sivan when Israel received the Torah"</span> — the revelation of <span class="h">chiddush ha'olam</span> [that Hashem created the world from nothing] = the foundation of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Lechem</span> = <span class="h">milchamah</span> [war]: <span class="v">"fight my wars [<span class="h">lachem es lachami</span>]."</span> The Zohar: <span class="mx">"the meal is the time of battle"</span> (as the child [<span class="h">Yenuka</span>] said). Bread = the Torah drawn through the <span class="h">tikkunim</span>, giving power to fight the obstructers of <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. The life-force received through eating = the strength to fight and subdue, to reach <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. <span class="kc">Only when one arrives at <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> is he called a warrior</span> — and this victory comes through Torah = bread.</div>

<!-- §23 -->
<div class="sn">§ 23 — The <i>Hei</i> of <i>HaMotzi</i>: Five Grains, Avraham's <i>Hei</i>, <i>Eretz Yisrael</i></div>

<div class="p">Our Sages emphasized: <span class="kc">pronounce the <span class="h">hei</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> distinctly.</span> The five species of grain with which <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span> was praised — upon which we bless <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> — are the aspect of the <span class="h">hei</span> of Avraham. Avraham was the first to reveal <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>'s holiness. He did not merit the Land until Hashem added <span class="h">hei</span> to his name: <span class="v">"your name shall no longer be called Avram; your name shall be Avraham… and I will give you and your children the land."</span></div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">hei</span> of <span class="h">HaMotzi</span> = the <span class="h">hei</span> of Avraham = the five grains = <span class="h">Eretz Yisrael</span>. All one.</div>

<!-- Translator's Summary -->
<div class="ts">
  <div class="sh">✦ Translator's Summary — Part 2 (§§ 13–23) ✦</div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§13 — Seven <em>Ushpizin</em></strong>: Each of the seven shepherds — Avraham, Yitzchak, Yaakov, Moshe, Aharon, Yosef, Dovid — revealed or conquered <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. They enter the <em>sukkah</em> because by their power we merit to enter the Land. Dovid (= Hoshana Rabbah, the seventh day) conquered it in fullness. The <em>sukkah</em> is therefore called "Dovid's <em>sukkah</em>." On Hoshana Rabbah: <em>Tehillim</em> (= mercy and supplication as a pauper) + <em>Mishneh Torah</em> (= every <em>parshah</em> speaks of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>).</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§14 — <em>Aravos</em> beaten alone</strong>: Until now, the wicked were rectified only when bound to the <em>tzadik</em> (<em>aravah</em> bound to <em>lulav</em>). Now on Hoshana Rabbah they stand alone — the <em>tzadik</em> has so thoroughly rectified them that they return through their own free choice. The beating sweetens residual judgments. Through this, the <em>Shechinah</em> unites on Shemini Atzeres.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§15-16 — Shemini Atzeres / Simchas Torah</strong>: Ascending to <em>Eretz Yisrael</em> in joy. Reading <em>V'Zos HaBerachah</em> = Torah completion → <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. Moshe's passing = rectification of the rock's error. As <em>Mashiach</em> he will return, draw Torah through mercy, and bring all to <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§17-18 — Application to eating</strong>: All food holiness derives from <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. All <em>berachos</em> were derived from <em>Birchas HaMazon</em> — which blesses for <em>the Land</em>. Bread = Torah = water + fire (supplication + fiery words from the Upper Heart). Bread sustains the heart because its root is the Upper Heart. Ten <em>tikkunim</em> in the meal = ten levels of holiness in <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>.</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§19 — Shabbos and <em>Eretz Yisrael</em></strong>: Both are holy; both bring satiation. The <em>tzadik</em> (= Yosef) is the source of satiation. "<em>Tzadikim</em> shall inherit the Land."</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§20 — Bread exempts all</strong>: Bread = the <em>tzadik</em> (the essential); other foods = the many souls he connects with. During a meal, all are secondary to bread. Alone, we bless <em>Borei Nefashos Rabbos</em> — "who creates many souls."</p>

  <p style="margin-bottom:.8rem;"><strong>§§21-23 — Tens and the <em>hei</em></strong>: Ten <em>mitzvos</em> in bread, ten fingers, ten words in <em>HaMotzi</em>, ten <em>tikkunim</em> in the meal = ten levels of <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>. <em>Lechem</em> = <em>milchamah</em> — bread is warfare for the Land. The <em>hei</em> of <em>HaMotzi</em> = the <em>hei</em> of Avraham = five grains = <em>Eretz Yisrael</em>.</p>
